"""`seeded_tree` gives each class the fixture, and takes back what the last one did. Needs Docker.

Without a consumer this fixture would be untested code shipping as infrastructure — and the way
it fails is the way that is hardest to notice: a class quietly inherits the previous class's
edits, its assertions still pass because they were written against the tree it *expected*, and
the first symptom is a matrix that only reds when its file is run alone.

So the classes below are ordered on purpose and the second one depends on the first having made
a mess. `TestAClassThatDirtiesTheTree` rewrites a surname over raw REST — not through an MCP tool,
because the point is the reseed, not the product — and `TestTheNextClassGetsItBack` asserts the
cast value is back *and* that the seeder had to do work for it. Asserting only the value would be
green on a fixture that reseeds unconditionally, which is the other failure: 14 s of wipe and
import per class, paid by every read-only group in the suite.

`TestAnOrderedChain` is the D5 property the matrices are built on: methods of one class share
`seeded_tree.state`, because a case captures an id the next case edits.
"""

